Redis-server1 & Redis-server2
----

Objetive:
    Transport data between scripts locally using RAM memory.

Technology stack used:
    redis
    Ubuntu
    Systemd-linux

Structure
    Build Redis server by binding it to a host and IP
    Create a stream channel
    Producer Push data
    Consumer pulls data

Breakdown
    Two servers were created with different ports:
    Redis-server1 
        - Port : 6381
        - Host : 127.0.0.1
    Redis-server2
        - Port : 6380
        - Host : 127.0.0.1

SystemD
    Both are managed and starte by systemD, they are services
    They are fired on everytime ubuntu is on
    Ubuntu will restart them if they crash

Files created

1) You created two custom systemd services
    Where what systemd uses to run the files and allow it to run/stop them
    /etc/systemd/system/redis-server1.service
    /etc/systemd/system/redis-server2.service


2) You created two separate Redis config folders
    These control everything important.
    /etc/redis/redis1/redis.conf
    /etc/redis/redis2/redis.conf

3) Runtime files (created at service start)
    These are temporary and live in RAM-backed dirs (usually).
    /var/run/redis/redis-server1.pid
    /var/run/redis/redis-server2.pid

4) Log files 
    Where Redis writes logs
    /var/log/redis/redis1/redis.log
    /var/log/redis/redis2/redis.log

5) Data persistence files 
    This is your actual “Redis database”
    /var/lib/redis/redis1/
    /var/lib/redis/redis2/

These live in the dir ... folder inside each Redis config.

Check their status
    systemctl status redis-server1

One liner to check all folder locations
    sudo egrep -n '^(port|bind|daemonize|pidfile|logfile|dir|appendonly|appendfilename|dbfilename)' /etc/redis/redis1/redis.conf
    sudo egrep -n '^(port|bind|daemonize|pidfile|logfile|dir|appendonly|appendfilename|dbfilename)' /etc/redis/redis2/redis.conf


Some command Summary
    cat file → show everything
    grep something file → show only lines containing “something” (need to put location and -option/flag to run it)
    grep -E / egrep → grep but with more powerful patterns